---
import ToolShell from "../../components/ToolShell.astro";
import { payeCalculate, payeCompare, payeBandBreakdown, payeBands2026 } from "../../lib/salary-tax";
import { formatGrouped } from "../../lib/format";
import { articleTitleOf } from "../../data/content";
const n = (v: number, d = 0) => formatGrouped(v, d);
const seed = payeCalculate(300000, 0.08, 0, 0, true);
const cmp = payeCompare(300000, 0.08, 0, 0);
const bands = payeBandBreakdown(seed.taxable, payeBands2026());
const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "")}/`;
// Related-guide card titles derive from the article records (SITE-3) ;  an
// unknown slug throws at build rather than shipping a stale card.
const artTitle = await articleTitleOf();
// Crossover-aware winner: the old bands beat the new ones well below executive
// pay (at ₦500k/mo, no rent, old wins ₦2,800/yr). Sub-naira gaps count as ties.
const saveA = cmp.save_annual;
const newWins = saveA > 0.5;
const oldWins = saveA < -0.5;
const tie = !newWins && !oldWins;
const saveTxt = tie
  ? "Both regimes leave you with the same take-home."
  : newWins
    ? `You keep ₦${n(cmp.save_monthly)}/mo more under the new bands.`
    : `Under the old bands you would have kept ₦${n(-cmp.save_monthly)}/mo more; the new bands are the only ones in force.`;
/* S5 item 2 ; the quick-check worked answers. Same engine, same 8%-pension /
   no-rent basis as the copy prose below (and as WP's server-computed answers
   at page-salary-tax-calculator.php:245-268), but computed HERE from the
   port's own payeCalculate() so the figures can never drift from the maths:
   nothing is hand-typed. These are labelled reference answers, not the
   visitor's receipt ; the receipt above still waits on his own numbers. */
const wk = [300000, 500000, 1000000].map((gross) => ({ gross, r: payeCalculate(gross, 0.08, 0, 0, true) }));
/* Chip labels: ₦300k / ₦500k / ₦1m ; the same shorthand the copy prose and
   WP's chips use. */
const wkLabel = (gross: number) => `₦${n(gross)}`;
---
<ToolShell title="Nigeria Tax Calculator 2026, NTA 2025 vs Old Bands - GWill Finance" description="Nigerian PAYE under the NTA 2025 bands (in force 1 Jan 2026) vs the old PITA bands, for comparison only (repealed 1 Jan 2026): rent relief, minimum-wage exemption, old-vs-new comparison." slug="salary-tax-calculator" tool="Nigeria Tax Calculator 2026" crumb="Nigeria Tax Calculator" lede="2026 tax rates: 0% under ₦800,000, then 15–25%. The old Personal Income Tax Act (PITA) rates are shown for comparison only. Rent relief cuts the income you pay tax on by 20% of your yearly rent; pay at or below the ₦70,000 minimum wage is tax-free." verified="NTA 2025 bands · rent relief capped ₦500,000 · exemption ≤ ₦70,000/mo (national minimum wage, source fmino.gov.ng) · last checked 25 Nov 2026" faqs={[
  { q: "What should I enter as my monthly pay before tax?", a: "Enter the gross pay on your payslip, before pension or tax is taken off: basic salary plus housing and transport allowances. Do not enter your take-home (net) pay; the tax and pension are worked out from the gross." },
  { q: "Why doesn't this match my payslip?", a: "This is a one-employer estimate. A payslip can differ because payroll applies reliefs in its own order, a bonus is taxed when it is paid, or you have a second job or other income. Check the gross, pension and deduction figures on your payslip; ask a tax professional for a final figure." },
  { q: "Who pays zero PAYE?", a: "PAYE is the income tax your employer withholds from salary. You pay none if your gross employment income is at or below the national minimum wage (Nigeria Tax Act 2025, s.162(1)(t)). This is different from the ₦800,000 zero-rate band, which applies to your taxable income once you are taxable. Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · <a href=\"https://nass.gov.ng/documents/download/11249\">Nigeria Tax Act 2025 gazette</a> s.162(1)(t), s.58 and Fourth Schedule" },
  { q: "How does rent relief work?", a: "Enter rent for the whole year. If you pay monthly, multiply by 12: ₦60,000 a month is ₦720,000 a year. 20% of the yearly rent reduces the income you pay tax on, capped at ₦500,000 a year. Keep rent receipts. Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · <a href=\"https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/\">Nigeria Tax Act 2025 s.30(2)(a)(vi)</a>" },
  { q: "What does 'income you pay tax on' mean?", a: "It is your yearly gross after the pension, NHF, NHIS, life insurance and rent relief you entered. It is not your take-home pay; the tax bands apply to this figure." },
  { q: "How is a bonus taxed?", a: "A bonus is added to your pay and taxed like extra salary; only the extra tax is charged, at the rate your last naira would pay. If it lifts your gross above the national minimum wage, the minimum-wage exemption no longer applies, but the first ₦800,000 of taxable income is still tax-free. It is taxed on the day it is paid (NTA 2025 ss.4(2)(a), 26(2)). Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · <a href=\"https://nass.gov.ng/documents/download/11249\">Nigeria Tax Act 2025 gazette</a>" },
  { q: "I'm self-employed, a trader or retired. Can I use this?", a: "This calculator estimates PAYE on salary from an employer. It does not model business profit, trading income, freelance fees or pension income: those are taxed under different rules, so do not enter them as gross salary. Ask a tax professional or your tax office how they apply to you." },
  { q: "Old vs new: who wins?", a: "The new bands win for most salaries, especially with rent relief. Without rent, the old bands would have come out slightly ahead for gross pay of roughly ₦325,000–₦535,000 a month (by at most about ₦1,000 a month), and again for very high earners. Note the old row is for comparison only: the Personal Income Tax Act it came from was repealed on 1 Jan 2026 (NTA 2025 s.195(g)), so no one is actually paid on it. Enter your salary and the comparison card shows your actual winner. Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · <a href=\"https://nass.gov.ng/documents/download/11249\">Nigeria Tax Act 2025 gazette</a>: s.195(g); old bands: <a href=\"https://fctirs.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Personal-Income-Tax-Act.pdf\">PITA Cap. P8 LFN 2004</a>" }
]}
  rawFaq
  badge="Nigeria Tax Act 2025 · In force since 1 Jan 2026",
  ctaTitle="What next",
  ctaNote="If you rent, your annual rent reduces taxable income: 20% of it is deducted, capped at ₦500,000 a year. Then confirm the pension basis and rent relief with payroll or HR: this is guidance, not tax advice.",
  ctas={[{ label: "Work backwards from take-home", href: `${base}money-tools/gross-to-net-calculator/` }, { label: "Plan this take-home 50-30-20", href: `${base}money-tools/50-30-20-budget-calculator/`, ghost: true }]},
  copy={[["p", "₦243,820 a month, that is the new take-home under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025 for a ₦300,000 gross salary at the minimum 8% pension. Use the calculator with your own numbers and rent to get an estimate."],["p", "₦394,700 a month on a ₦500,000 gross at the minimum pension. Add your annual rent above to see how rent relief reduces your taxable income further."],["p", "₦771,900 a month on a ₦1,000,000 gross at the minimum pension. The old-vs-new comparison card above shows your exact savings under the new law."],["p", "Your annual gross is reduced by pension, NHF, NHIS, life assurance and rent relief (20% of annual rent, capped at ₦500,000). What remains is your taxable income, which is taxed on graduated bands, 0% on the first ₦800,000, then 15% up to ₦3,000,000, 18% up to ₦12,000,000, 21% up to ₦25,000,000, 23% up to ₦50,000,000 and 25% above that."],["p", "The Nigeria Tax Act 2025 replaces the old Consolidated Relief Allowance (₦200,000 + 20% of gross) with a rent relief of 20% of your annual rent, capped at ₦500,000. If you pay ₦2,400,000 a year in rent, you get ₦480,000 off your taxable income. Enter your annual rent above to see the effect."],["p", "The Nigeria Tax Act 2025 (NTA 2025), in force since 1 January 2026, is the biggest change to personal income tax in Nigeria in decades. It was designed to leave the lowest earners completely untaxed, widen the tax-free threshold for everyone, and simplify how allowances work, and for most workers it results in more take-home pay."],["h3", "The new 0% band, most people now start at zero"],["p", "Under the old law, tax started from the first naira of taxable income at 7%. NTA 2025 sets a 0% band on the first ₦800,000 of annual taxable income, so a big slice of every salary is now completely tax-free before any higher bands apply. You pay no income tax on an employment if your gross income from that employment is at or below the national minimum wage (Nigeria Tax Act 2025, s.162(1)(t)). This is a different thing from the ₦800,000 zero-rate band, which applies to your taxable income once you are taxable. Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · Nigeria Tax Act 2025 s.162(1)(t), s.58 and Fourth Schedule"],["h3", "Rent relief replaces the old allowance"],["p", "The old Consolidated Relief Allowance, the flat ₦200,000 plus 20% of gross income that everyone claimed, is gone. In its place is a rent relief worth 20% of your annual rent, capped at ₦500,000. That is a real shift: someone who pays no rent (living rent-free or in staff quarters) no longer gets an automatic allowance, while tenants get a deduction that scales with what they actually pay. This is one of the biggest reasons your take-home under the new law can differ from the old figures."],["h3", "Wider bands, higher top rate"],["p", "The tax bands are now far wider, 15% on income up to ₦3,000,000, 18% up to ₦12,000,000, 21% up to ₦25,000,000, 23% up to ₦50,000,000 and 25% above that, so your effective rate stays lower for longer. Only the very highest earners reach the new 25% top rate (up from 24% before)."],["p", "Type your salary and rent into the calculator above and compare the old and new rows to see exactly what the law change means for your personal take-home, every figure is computed live from the official bands."],["h3", "Nigerian tax calculator questions"],["h3", "What changed in the Nigeria Tax Act 2025?"],["p", "The first ₦800,000 of annual income is now tax-free (0% band). The old Consolidated Relief Allowance (CRA) is replaced by a rent relief of 20% of annual rent, capped at ₦500,000. Employees earning ₦70,000 a month or less pay zero PAYE (the national minimum wage figure, per the Federal Ministry of Information). The top rate is now 25% instead of 24%."],["h3", "How do I claim rent relief?"],["p", "Enter your annual rent in the calculator. The relief is automatically applied, 20% of your annual rent, up to ₦500,000. Your employer would normally adjust your PAYE for this in the official filing."],["h3", "Did the NRS replace the FIRS, and when do the new tax rules start?"],["p", "The Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS) replaced the Federal Inland Revenue Service under an Act that took effect on 26 June 2025. The new tax rules themselves (the Nigeria Tax Act 2025) take effect from 1 January 2026. Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS) Establishment Act 2025 gazette (Official Gazette No. 117, 26 Jun 2025), covering commencement on 1 January 2026 · Aluko & Oyebode transition overview (further reading only, not authority for a section number)"],["h3", "What pension rate should I use?"],["p", "Your pension contribution is a minimum of 8% of your monthly emoluments (that is your basic salary plus your housing and transport allowances), while your employer adds a minimum of 10%, under the Pension Reform Act 2014. Deductions you claim must be made in writing to your tax authority. Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · PenCom FAQ (pencom.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/FAQ-CPS-reviewed.-17-Apr.-2018.pdf) · NTA s.31 on written claims"],["h3", "What is the NHF limit?"],["p", "The National Housing Fund contribution is 2.5% of your basic monthly salary, uncapped: the NHF Act sets no ceiling on the amount (confirm the figure your payroll applies). Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · NHF Act ss.4(a), 9(1) (Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria) · NTA s.30(2)(a)(i)"]]},
  related={[{ href: `${base}articles/kuda-vs-traditional-banks-charges/`, title: artTitle("kuda-vs-traditional-banks-charges"), excerpt: "Card fees, failed-transfer reversals, and support tested: what digital banks actually save you." }]},
  disclaimer="This calculator is for informational guidance only and is not tax, financial or investment advice. Tax rules and personal circumstances vary, confirm your figures with a licensed tax professional.">
<div class="fx-card">
<p class="hint st-scope"><strong>Salary/employment PAYE only.</strong> Business, freelance and pension income are not covered.</p>
<p class="echo" data-sf-card hidden>Nothing here is filled in for you. Enter your numbers and the receipt waits.</p>
<!-- PERIOD TOGGLE (S5 item 5) ; the WP tax tools' Monthly/Annual switch
     (page-salary-tax-calculator.php:68-71, with the label-flip JS at
     assets/js/salary-tax.js:331-345), in the port's own segmented grammar
     (.fx-stepper/.fx-step, the control the savings-goal mode switch uses).
     DISPLAY-ONLY: the engine and every input stay monthly, and the per-year
     view reads the engine's OWN annual fields where they exist (net_annual,
     tax_annual, save_annual ; they do on this page), so no figure is a
     multiplied guess. -->
<div class="fx-stepper" role="group" aria-label="Receipt period">
  <button type="button" class="fx-step active" data-st-period="monthly" aria-pressed="true">Monthly</button>
  <button type="button" class="fx-step" data-st-period="annual" aria-pressed="false">Annual</button>
</div>
<!-- E7 F6 ; compact in-card jump row. Four 44px targets in the lower third of
     the viewport at scroll 0, so a one-handed edit no longer starts with a
     long hunt. Each button scrolls its own control into view; none writes a
     figure, changes state, or steals focus except Gross, which focuses the
     field a thumb just chose. Gaps are wider than the 8px touch-slop floor. -->
<div class="st-jump" role="group" aria-label="Jump to calculator controls">
  <button type="button" class="st-jump-btn" data-st-jump="gross" aria-label="Jump to monthly gross field">Gross</button>
  <button type="button" class="st-jump-btn" data-st-jump="period" aria-label="Jump to monthly or annual period control">Period</button>
  <button type="button" class="st-jump-btn" data-st-jump="result" aria-label="Jump to take-home result">Result</button>
  <button type="button" class="st-jump-btn" data-st-jump="clear" aria-label="Jump to clear and reset controls">Clear</button>
</div>
<p class="hint">Only the take-home figure, tax line, result and comparison table change with this switch. The four rows below (pension, other deductions, rent relief and PAYE, pay-as-you-earn tax) stay yearly.</p>
<p class="hint" id="st-legend">Marked <strong>Sample</strong> means the figure is our worked example, not your own: type over any field and its Sample marker disappears. Clear examples removes only what is still ours; numbers you typed stay.</p>
<p class="hint" id="st-rule">Fill all eight fields - type 0 for anything you do not have.</p>
<div class="tgrid c3">

<div class="field" data-cur="NGN"><label for="st-gross">Monthly pay before tax ₦</label><span class="fi"><span class="fi-sym" aria-hidden="true">₦</span><input id="st-gross" type="text" value="300000" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-gross-hint"></span><p class="hint" id="st-gross-hint">Enter your monthly pay before tax. This is the gross pay on your payslip: basic salary plus housing and transport allowances. The 2026 tax rates are worked out from this figure.</p></div>
<div class="field"><label for="st-pension">Pension contribution %</label><input id="st-pension" type="text" max="100" value="8" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-pension-hint"><p class="hint" id="st-pension-hint">Enter the pension % on your payslip. Leave 8 if you are not sure. Pension is calculated on basic salary plus housing and transport allowances: at least 8% from you and 10% from your employer. Only what you pay is deductible under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025.<br><em>Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · NTA <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/">s.30(2)(a)(iii)</a> · <a href="https://www.pencom.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/FAQ-CPS-reviewed.-17-Apr.-2018.pdf">PenCom FAQ on the contributory pension scheme</a> (8% employee, 10% employer minimum)</em></p></div>
<div class="field" data-cur="NGN"><label for="st-rent">Annual rent (12 months) ₦</label><span class="fi"><span class="fi-sym" aria-hidden="true">₦</span><input id="st-rent" type="text" value="0" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-rent-hint st-rent-unit"></span><p class="hint" id="st-rent-hint">Enter rent for the whole year. If you pay monthly, multiply by 12: ₦60,000 a month is ₦720,000 a year. 20% of the yearly rent comes off the income you pay tax on, capped at ₦500,000.<br><em>Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · NTA <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/">s.30(2)(a)(vi)</a></em></p><p class="hint" id="st-rent-unit" hidden></p></div>
<div class="field" data-cur="NGN"><label for="st-nhf">Housing fund monthly ₦</label><span class="fi"><span class="fi-sym" aria-hidden="true">₦</span><input id="st-nhf" type="text" value="0" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-nhf-hint"></span><p class="hint" id="st-nhf-hint">Enter the housing fund (NHF) amount on your payslip each month. Leave 0 if none. It is usually 2.5% of basic pay and comes off the income you pay tax on.<br><em>Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · <a href="https://fmbn.gov.ng/backend/storage/uploads/37UNgpzsyASabzhQCnHuyzFtIpnsfmW2ODvAmQSG.pdf">NHF Act s.4</a> (2.5% of basic monthly salary) · NTA <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/">s.30(2)(a)(i)</a></em></p></div>
<div class="field" data-cur="NGN"><label for="st-nhis">Health insurance monthly ₦</label><span class="fi"><span class="fi-sym" aria-hidden="true">₦</span><input id="st-nhis" type="text" value="0" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-nhis-hint"></span><p class="hint" id="st-nhis-hint">Enter the health insurance (NHIS) amount on your payslip each month. Leave 0 if none. It comes off the income you pay tax on.<br><em>Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · NTA <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/">s.30(2)(a)(ii)</a></em></p></div>
<div class="field" data-cur="NGN"><label for="st-life">Life insurance monthly ₦</label><span class="fi"><span class="fi-sym" aria-hidden="true">₦</span><input id="st-life" type="text" value="0" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-life-hint"></span><p class="hint" id="st-life-hint">Enter life insurance from your payslip each month. Leave 0 if none. It comes off the income you pay tax on.<br><em>Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · NTA <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/">s.30(2)(a)(v)</a></em></p></div>
<div class="field" data-cur="NGN"><label for="st-other">Other deductions monthly ₦</label><span class="fi"><span class="fi-sym" aria-hidden="true">₦</span><input id="st-other" type="text" value="0" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-other-hint"></span><p class="hint" id="st-other-hint">Enter any other deduction from your payslip each month. Leave 0 if none. It reduces the income you pay tax on. Same field as our Net to Gross calculator.<br><em>Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · NTA <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/">s.30(2)(a)</a></em></p></div>
<div class="field" data-cur="NGN"><label for="st-bonus">Bonus / 13th month (an extra month's pay) ₦</label><span class="fi"><span class="fi-sym" aria-hidden="true">₦</span><input id="st-bonus" type="text" value="0" inputmode="decimal" aria-describedby="st-bonus-hint"></span><p class="hint" id="st-bonus-hint">Enter any one-off bonus or 13th-month pay. Leave 0 if none. It is taxed on the day it is paid. The bonus is added to your pay and only the extra tax is charged, at the rate on your last naira.<br><em>Last checked 25 Nov 2026 · NTA ss.4(2)(a), 26(2), 162(1)(t) in the <a href="https://nass.gov.ng/documents/download/11249">Nigeria Tax Act 2025 gazette</a></em></p></div>
</div>
<p class="hint" id="st-dedwarn" hidden></p>
<div class="receipt" data-share-note="Estimate, not tax advice. Nigeria Tax Act 2025, checked 25 Nov 2026.">
<p class="receipt-lbl" id="st-lbl">Take-home monthly</p>
<p class="receipt-fig" id="st-fig" aria-live="polite" aria-label={`Take-home: ₦${n(seed.net_monthly)} a month`}><span id="st-fig-v" aria-hidden="true">₦{n(seed.net_monthly)}</span></p>
<p class="receipt-sub" id="st-sub">Tax ₦{n(seed.tax_monthly)} a month · income you pay tax on ₦{n(seed.taxable)} a year{seed.exempt ? " · no tax (at or below minimum wage)" : ""}</p>
<p class="hint" id="st-next"><strong>What next:</strong> spending this take-home? <a class="st-next-link" href={`${base}money-tools/50-30-20-budget-calculator/`}>Plan it with the 50-30-20 budget</a>. Chasing a target take-home? <a class="st-next-link" href={`${base}money-tools/gross-to-net-calculator/`}>Work back to the gross you need</a>.</p>
<p class="receipt-foot" id="st-memory-note">Only your pension rate is remembered on this page. Gross, rent, NHF, NHIS, life assurance, other reliefs and bonus are never saved, so they return to the worked example.</p>
<p class="receipt-foot" id="st-mixed" hidden></p>
<!-- E8b M1-M4 / L1-L3 ; the legal basis and the limits sit BESIDE the figure.
     These are the public documents verified in the E8a source map. Section
     numbers stay as text and follow the printed gazette numbering. -->
<div class="st-trust" id="st-trust">
<p class="st-trust-line st-seed-teach" id="st-seed-teach"><strong>What this example includes:</strong> gross ₦300,000 a month, pension at 8% (the statutory minimum), and ₦0 in every other field: rent, NHF, NHIS, life assurance, other reliefs and bonus. Add your annual rent to see rent relief cut your taxable income and move your take-home, or fill NHF, NHIS or life assurance to see a deduction move it. The bonus is left at ₦0 here, so no separate bonus tax shows; enter a bonus and it is taxed on its own at your marginal band.</p>
<p class="st-trust-line st-last-change" id="st-last-change" hidden></p>
<p class="st-trust-line"><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://nass.gov.ng/documents/download/11249">Nigeria Tax Act 2025 gazette</a> (ss.4(2)(a), 26(2), 162(1)(t), 195(g)) and the <a href="https://nipc.gov.ng/assets/resources/nigeria-tax-act-2025/nigeria-tax-act-2025-gazette.pdf">gazette copy with the band table</a> (s.58 and Fourth Schedule); <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/30/">s.30(2)(a)</a> (rent (vi), pension (iii), NHF (i), NHIS (ii), life (v)) and <a href="https://repo.podus.ai/laws/nigeria-tax-act/section/31/">s.31</a> (reliefs claimed in writing); <a href="https://nipc.gov.ng/assets/resources/nigeria-revenue-service-establishment-act-2025/nigeria-revenue-service-establishment-act-2025-gazette.pdf">Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS) Establishment Act 2025</a>; <a href="https://fmbn.gov.ng/backend/storage/uploads/37UNgpzsyASabzhQCnHuyzFtIpnsfmW2ODvAmQSG.pdf">NHF Act</a>; <a href="https://www.pencom.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/FAQ-CPS-reviewed.-17-Apr.-2018.pdf">PenCom FAQ</a>; <a href="https://fctirs.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Personal-Income-Tax-Act.pdf">PITA, old bands only</a>. <a href="https://aluko-oyebode.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/OVERVIEW-OF-THE-GENERAL-TRANSITION-GUIDELINES-FOR-THE-NEW-TAX-ACTS-2025.pdf">Aluko &amp; Oyebode</a> is further reading, not authority for a section number.</p>
<p class="st-trust-line"><strong>Minimum wage:</strong> no PAYE on an employment at or below the national minimum wage, currently ₦70,000 a month, per the <a href="https://fmino.gov.ng/federal-government-inaugurates-committee-on-consequential-salaries-adjustments/">Federal Ministry of Information and National Orientation</a>; the exemption itself is s.162(1)(t) of the gazette.</p>
<p class="st-trust-line"><strong>Method and disclaimer:</strong> an estimate for guidance, not tax advice. We apply the 2026 bands to the annual gross you enter, subtract the deductions you enter, and show annual figures in the receipt. The full disclaimer and article follow below.</p>
<p class="st-trust-line"><strong>Why ₦300,000:</strong> it is our worked example because it clears the ₦800,000 zero-rate band and reaches the 15% band, so the receipt shows a non-zero tax line without any deduction we invented. The 8% pension is the statutory minimum employee contribution. The ₦0 in every other field is a placeholder, not a claim about your payslip. Type over it with your own pay.</p>
<p class="st-trust-line"><strong>Estimate, not your payslip:</strong> PAYE is withheld when income is paid, so a one-off bonus is taxed on its payment day; allowances and bonuses change which bands apply; NHF, NHIS, life assurance and other reliefs are written up by your payroll; and a second employer is assessed separately, so this one-employer estimate will not match a two-job payslip.</p>
<p class="st-trust-line st-trust-checked"><strong>Last checked 25 Nov 2026</strong> against the public sources linked above.</p>
</div>
<p class="receipt-foot st-own" id="st-own-rec" data-own>Ours, not yours: our worked example, not your salary.</p>
<div class="receipt-div" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div id="st-rows">
<div class="receipt-row"><span>Pension (annual)</span><b id="st-pen">₦{n(seed.pension_annual)}</b></div>
<!-- E8b H1 ; the three deductions the engine subtracted but the receipt never
     printed. Hidden while zero so the seeded example stays clean; sfFig still
